{"records":[{"id":"t573dphyc5","title":"iPSC-derived microglia export cholesterol to neuronal cells in human brain organoids and promote their maturation","description":"Microglia are specialised brain-resident macrophages that arise from primitive macrophages colonising the embryonic brain 1. Microglia contribute to multiple aspects of brain development, but their precise roles in early human brain remain poorly understood due to limited access to relevant tissues2-6. The generation of brain organoids from induced human pluripotent stem cells (iPSC) recapitulates some key features of human embryonic brain development7-10. However, current approaches do not incorporate microglia or did not address their role in organoid maturation11-21. Here, we generated microglia-sufficient brain organoids by co-culturing brain organoids with primitive-like macrophages generated from the same human iPSC (iMac)22. In organoid co-cultures, iMac differentiated into cells with microglia-like phenotypes and functions (iMicro), and modulated neuronal progenitor cell (NPC) differentiation, limiting NPC proliferation and promoting axonogenesis. Mechanistically, iMicro contained high levels of PLIN2+ lipid droplets that exported cholesterol and its esters which were taken up by NPC in the organoids. We also detected PLIN2+ lipid droplet-loaded microglia in mouse and human embryonic brain. Overall, our approach significantly advances current human brain organoid approaches by incorporating microglial cells, illustrated by the discovery of a key pathway of lipid-mediated crosstalk between microglia and NPC leading to improved neurogenesis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-29T14:24:34.021Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t573dphyc5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t573dphyc5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Wai","last_name":"tham","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liang","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lipidomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9dsmdrcfd7","title":"Microglia-specific overexpression of α-synuclein leads to severe dopaminergic neurodegeneration by phagocytic exhaustion and oxidative toxicity","description":"Recent findings in human samples and animal models support the involvement of inflammation in the development of Parkinson’s disease. Nevertheless, it is currently unknown whether microglial activation constitutes a primary event in neurodegeneration. We generated a new mouse model by lentiviral-mediated selective α-synuclein (αSYN) accumulation in microglial cells. Surprisingly, these mice developed progressive degeneration of dopaminergic (DA) neurons without endogenous αSYN aggregation. Transcriptomics and functional assessment revealed that αSYN-accumulating microglial cells developed a strong reactive state with phagocytic exhaustion and excessive production of oxidative and proinflammatory molecules. This inflammatory state created a molecular feed-forward vicious cycle between microglia and IFNγ-secreting immune cells infiltrating the brain parenchyma. Pharmacological inhibition of oxidative and nitrosative molecule production was sufficient to attenuate neurodegeneration. These results suggest that αSYN accumulation in microglia induces selective DA neuronal degeneration by promoting phagocytic exhaustion, an excessively toxic environment and the selective recruitment of peripheral immune cells.","source":{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-15T06:29:24.175Z","url":"https://ordr.hsr.it/datasets/9dsmdrcfd7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9dsmdrcfd7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Vania","last_name":"Broccoli","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Ministero della Salute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Neuroscience","Neurodegenerative Disorder","Parkinson's Disease"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8j2gsn3bwv","title":"CReCOM Brain Macrophages","description":"A Novel Microglia-Speciﬁc Transcriptional Signature Correlates With Behavioral Deﬁcits in Neuropsychiatric Lupus, Frontiers in Immunology, Feb 2020. Neuropsychiatric symptoms of systemic lupus erythematosus (NP-SLE) affect over one-half of SLE patients, yet underlying mechanisms remain largely unknown. We isolated brain-infiltrating macrophages from aged SLE-prone mice (CReCOM, CD11cC8). We demonstrate that CReCOM mice develop NP-SLE, including behavioral deﬁcits prior to systemic autoimmunity, reduced brain volumes, decreased vascular integrity, and brain-inﬁltrating leukocytes. NP-SLE microglia exhibit numerical expansion, increased synaptic uptake, and a more metabolically active phenotype. Microglia from multiple SLE-prone models express a “NP-SLE signature” unrelated to type I interferon. Rather, the signature is associated with lipid metabolism, scavenger receptor activity and downregulation of inﬂammatory and chemotaxis processes, suggesting a more regulatory, anti-inﬂammatory proﬁle. NP-SLE microglia also express genes associated with disease-associated microglia (DAM), a subset of microglia thought to be instrumental in neurodegenerative diseases. Further, expression of “NP-SLE” and “DAM” signatures correlate with the severity of behavioral deﬁcits in young SLE-prone mice prior to overt systemic disease. Our data are the ﬁrst to demonstrate the predictive value of our newly identiﬁed microglia-speciﬁc “NP-SLE” and “DAM” signatures as a surrogate for NP-SLE clinical outcomes and suggests that microglia-intrinsic defects precede contributions from systemic SLE for neuropsychiatric manifestations.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-02-06T21:51:39.063Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8j2gsn3bwv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8j2gsn3bwv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Carla","last_name":"Cuda","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Brain","Systemic Lupus Erythematosus","Macrophage","Mouse Model"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gphwtzz36y","title":"CReCOM Microglia","description":"A Novel Microglia-Speciﬁc Transcriptional Signature Correlates With Behavioral Deﬁcits in Neuropsychiatric Lupus, Frontiers in Immunology, Feb 2020. Neuropsychiatric symptoms of systemic lupus erythematosus (NP-SLE) affect over one-half of SLE patients, yet underlying mechanisms remain largely unknown. We isolated microglia from young and aged SLE-prone mice (CReCOM, CD11cC8). We demonstrate that CReCOM mice develop NP-SLE, including behavioral deﬁcits prior to systemic autoimmunity, reduced brain volumes, decreased vascular integrity, and brain-inﬁltrating leukocytes. NP-SLE microglia exhibit numerical expansion, increased synaptic uptake, and a more metabolically active phenotype. Microglia from multiple SLE-prone models express a “NP-SLE signature” unrelated to type I interferon. Rather, the signature is associated with lipid metabolism, scavenger receptor activity and downregulation of inﬂammatory and chemotaxis processes, suggesting a more regulatory, anti-inﬂammatory proﬁle. NP-SLE microglia also express genes associated with disease-associated microglia (DAM), a subset of microglia thought to be instrumental in neurodegenerative diseases. Further, expression of “NP-SLE” and “DAM” signatures correlate with the severity of behavioral deﬁcits in young SLE-prone mice prior to overt systemic disease. Our data are the ﬁrst to demonstrate the predictive value of our newly identiﬁed microglia-speciﬁc “NP-SLE” and “DAM” signatures as a surrogate for NP-SLE clinical outcomes and suggests that microglia-intrinsic defects precede contributions from systemic SLE for neuropsychiatric manifestations.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-02-06T21:53:36.625Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gphwtzz36y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gphwtzz36y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Carla","last_name":"Cuda","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microglia","Systemic Lupus Erythematosus","Neuropsychiatric Disorder","Mouse Model"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pdt9bf5gyb","title":"Kinase Alpha 2 Acts as a Protein Kinase to Promote Lipolysis of Lipid Droplets","description":"Lipid droplets are important for cancer cell growth and survival. However, the mechanism underlying the initiation of lipid droplet lipolysis is not well understood. We demonstrate here that glucose deprivation induces the binding of choline kinase (CHK)2 to lipid droplets, which is sequentially mediated by AMPK-dependent CHK2 S279 phosphorylation and KAT5-dependent CHK2 K247 acetylation. Importantly, CHK2 with altered catalytic domain conformation functions as a protein kinase and phosphorylates PLIN2 at Y232 and PLIN3 at Y251. The phosphorylated PLIN2/3 dissociate from lipid droplets and are degraded by Hsc70-mediated autophagy, thereby promoting lipid droplet lipolysis, fatty acid oxidation, and brain tumor growth. In addition, the levels of CHK2 S279 phosphorylation, CHK2 K247 acetylation, and PLIN2/3 phosphorylation are positively correlated with each other in human glioblastoma specimens and are associated with poor prognosis in glioblastoma patients. These findings underscore the role of CHK2 as a protein kinase in lipolysis and glioblastoma development.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-29T14:25:50.506Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pdt9bf5gyb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pdt9bf5gyb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhimin","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"RUI","last_name":"LIU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vs8r8tbyn8","title":"Brain-engrafted monocyte-derived macrophages from blood and skull-bone marrow exhibit distinct properties [Dataset]","description":"The dataset includes a spreadsheet file containing all raw data for main and supplementary figures for the paper published on Neuron \"Brain-engrafted monocyte-derived macrophages from blood and skull-bone marrow exhibit distinct properties\". The main findings of the study are: repeated microglia depletion allows peripheral monocytes to engraft the brain; brain-engrafted macrophages acquire transcriptional and epigenetic identities distinct from microglia and adopt a border-associated macrophage-like phenotype; monocytes from both blood and skull origins replenish the brain, revealing unexpected routes of brain macrophage renewal. The data is in a simple Excel spreadsheet with 43 data tabs. A list of all data tabs is in the Master-README file.","source":{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-11-05T20:53:40.186Z","url":"https://digitalcommonsdata.wustl.edu/datasets/vs8r8tbyn8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vs8r8tbyn8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Siling","last_name":"Du","orcid":"0000-0003-1540-3585"},{"first_name":"Feiya","last_name":"Ou","orcid":"0000-0001-8289-4025"},{"first_name":"Antoine","last_name":"Drieu","orcid":"0000-0001-6237-4719"},{"first_name":"Eric Z.","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yumeng","last_name":"Cheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Steffen E.","last_name":"Storck","orcid":"0000-0002-6965-2264"},{"first_name":"Tornike","last_name":"Mamuladze","orcid":"0000-0002-7088-3967"},{"first_name":"Jay","last_name":"Cao","orcid":"0009-0007-2885-1711"},{"first_name":"Nora","last_name":"Abduljawad","orcid":"0000-0002-2220-1730"},{"first_name":"Bishan","last_name":"Bhattarai","orcid":"0009-0003-1263-8554"},{"first_name":"Justin","last_name":"Rustenhoven","orcid":"0000-0002-9376-7424"},{"first_name":"Niall","last_name":"Mortimer","orcid":"0000-0003-4608-2585"},{"first_name":"Simone","last_name":"Brioschi","orcid":"0000-0002-3849-3926"},{"first_name":"Khai","last_name":"Nguyen","orcid":"0000-0003-1298-6762"},{"first_name":"Patrick Fernandes","last_name":"Rodrigues","orcid":"0000-0002-0214-0627"},{"first_name":"Igor","last_name":"Smirnov","orcid":"0000-0003-2373-6742"},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Gibson","orcid":"0000-0002-1998-1289"},{"first_name":"J. Michael","last_name":"White","orcid":"0000-0001-8511-6026"},{"first_name":"Wandy","last_name":"Beatty","orcid":"0000-0003-0715-7361"},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"DeNardo","orcid":"0000-0002-3655-5783"},{"first_name":"Qingyun","last_name":"Li","orcid":"0000-0002-3457-5967"},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Meers","orcid":"0000-0003-3438-3938"},{"first_name":"Claudia Z.","last_name":"Han","orcid":"0000-0002-2285-5582"},{"first_name":"Na","last_name":"Sun","orcid":"0009-0008-5785-5495"},{"first_name":"Florent","last_name":"Ginhoux","orcid":"0000-0002-2857-7755"},{"first_name":"Marina","last_name":"Cella","orcid":"0000-0003-1230-1313"},{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Colonna","orcid":"0000-0001-5222-4987"},{"first_name":"Jonathan","last_name":"Kipnis","orcid":"0000-0002-3714-517X"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/049v75w11","name":"National Institute on Aging","grant_id":"P01AG078106"}],"categories":["Immunology"],"journal_issn":["08966273"]},{"id":"9nsk2tcjsz","title":"AutoSpectral: mouse brain imaging panel","description":"A mouse brain sample stained with a 10-colour flow panel for sorting microglia, oligodendrocytes and astrocytes on the FACSDiscover S8.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-24T23:14:07.264Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9nsk2tcjsz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9nsk2tcjsz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Oliver","last_name":"Burton","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adrian","last_name":"Liston","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/05f950310","name":"KU Leuven","grant_id":"C1 program"},{"id":"https://ror.org/029chgv08","name":"Wellcome Trust","grant_id":"222442/A/21/Z"},{"id":"https://ror.org/0472cxd90","name":"European Research Council","grant_id":"874707 (EXIMIOUS)"},{"id":"https://ror.org/001aqnf71","name":"UK Research and Innovation","grant_id":"Proactive Vaccinology Award MR/Y004450/1 (IMMPROVE)"}],"categories":["Flow Cytometry","Spectral Unmixing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j3hsymhvrj","title":"Uncovering the protective role of lipid droplet accumulation against acid-induced  oxidative stress and cell death in osteosarcoma","description":"Highlights\n•\tLipid droplet formation mitigates cellular stress\n•\tTargeting key proteins involved in lipid droplet formation leads to a significant decline in cell viability and increased ROS production\n•\tLactate is a key metabolite in lipid droplet formation\n•\tLipid droplet biogenesis is fuelled by tumor-associated stromal cells\n\nFiles show ROS quantification, measured on plate reader, by means of DCF fluorescence. \nThe file named ROS time points, shows raw data and graph of ROS measurment in 143B cultured in normal 7.4 medium (time point 0) or in acid 6.8 medium at different time points (2, 7 and 24h). ROS were identified by the 2′,7′-dichlorofluorescin (DCFH) method. In summary, cells were cultured in complete medium at 7.4 or 6.8 pH. At the indicated time points, cells were washed and then incubated with 10 μM 5- and 6-carboxy-2′,7′-dichlorodihydrofluorescein diacetate (CM-H2DCFDA; Thermo Fisher Scientific, Monza, Italy) for 5 minutes at 37 °C. The fluorescent signal of DCF resulting from the transformation of 2′,7′-dichlorofluorescin-diacetate (DCFH-DA) by intracellular ROS production was measured at 485–535 nm using a microplate reader (Tecan Infinite F200pro, Mannedorf, Switerzland). The results were expressed as the mean of the relative fluorescence units (RFU).\n\nThe file named ROS with siRNA shows ROS quantification after silencing of DGAT1 or PLIN2, two key components of lipid droplet formation. Silenced cells were transferred into 500 uL/well of pen/strep-free medium in 96-well plates for ROS quantification (5,000 cells/well). 24 hours after electroporation, cells were transferred in complete medium at 7.4 or 6.8 pH for the indicated times. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-09T13:01:51.203Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j3hsymhvrj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j3hsymhvrj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nicola","last_name":"Baldini","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Margherita","last_name":"Cortini","orcid":"0000-0002-8151-8105"},{"first_name":"Sofia","last_name":"Avnet","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"http://dx.doi.org/10.13039/100020581","name":"Fondazione AIRC per la ricerca sul cancro ETS","grant_id":"ID. 21403"}],"categories":["Osteosarcoma","Microenvironment","Reactive Oxygen Species","Acidosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gsd5hxdphy","title":"Influence of glial biotin availability on coping up with ER stress ","description":"We hypothesize that biotin favors BCAA utilization and its deficiency accumulates BCAA to prolong autophagy inhibition and ER stress by chronic activation of mTORC1. \n\nGanesan, D, Ramaian Santhaseela, A, Rajasekaran, S, Selvam, S, Jayavelu, T. Astroglial biotin deprivation under endoplasmic reticulum stress uncouples BCAA‐mTORC1 role in lipid synthesis to prolong autophagy inhibition in the aging brain. J. Neurochem. 2020; 00: 1– 14. https://doi.org/10.1111/jnc.14979","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-03-08T16:31:09.45Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gsd5hxdphy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gsd5hxdphy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dhasarathan","last_name":"Ganesan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tamilselvan","last_name":"Jayavelu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Glia","Biotin","Cholesterol","Triacylglycerol","Mammalian Target of Rapamycin","Aging","Autophagy","Cellular Signaling","Insulin","Endoplasmic Reticulum Stress","Glial Cells"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zczszym8vt","title":"Aging features of the migratory locust at physiological and transcriptional levels","description":"Background\nNon-Drosophila insects provide diverse aging types and important complementary systems for studies of aging biology. However, little attention has been paid to the special roles of non-Drosophila insects in aging research. Here, the aging-related features of the migratory locust, Locusta migratoria, were determined at the physiological, cellular, and transcriptional levels.\nResults\nIn physiological assessments, the flight performance and sperm state of locusts displayed clear aging-related decline in male adults. At the cellular level, the incidence of mitochondrial abnormalities significantly increased in aged flight muscle, and apoptotic signals and nuclear abnormalities were enhanced in aged fat body but not in brain. Furthermore, organ-level transcriptional analyses demonstrated locusts have similar aging-related genes with model species and the unique organ-specific aging features. Different from those of Drosophila and mammals, the organ-specific aging features of locusts were characterized by intensive expression changes in flight muscle and fat body and little transcriptional and physiological changes in brain. Upregulation of genes involved in autophagy in flight muscle and detoxification in fat body suggested remarkable damage accumulation in old locusts. Some well-known aging genes and locust aging-related genes (i.e., IAP1, PGRP-SA, and LIPT1), whose roles in aging regulation were rarely reported, were demonstrated to affect lifespan, metabolism, and flight ability of locusts after RNAi.\nConclusion\nThis study revealed multi-level aging signatures of locust, thus laying a foundation for further investigation of aging mechanisms in this famous insect in the future.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-19T05:21:14.582Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zczszym8vt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zczszym8vt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Siyuan","last_name":"Guo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biologicals"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":141888,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":196,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":141888,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":141066,"restricted":72,"embargoed":750},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2342},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":708},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":480},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":452},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":442},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":424},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":404},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":338},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":282},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":234}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":139214},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1915},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":187},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":141},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":44},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":541},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":408},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":376},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":361},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":330}]}}